1. The subscription problem
A fitness app might sell Premium workout plans, Pro coaching, or advanced progress analytics. A payment callback does not prove an account should retain access forever. Renewals, grace periods, refunds, upgrades, account recovery, and missed notifications all change the answer.

Keep these concepts separate:
Concept	Meaning	Example
Store product	Purchasable item configured with a store	fit_pro_monthly
Purchase/transaction	Evidence of a store operation	Apple transaction ID; Google purchase token
Subscription state	Store-reported lifecycle status	Active, grace, on hold, expired
Entitlement	Your product's authorization decision	coaching: true


Invariant: stores are authoritative for the underlying purchases; the backend entitlement service is authoritative for your app's protected features. Clients display access, but may not grant server-side access by toggling local flags.
2. Entitlements and lifecycle states
Define plan-to-feature mapping in one place. Do not scatter store product IDs across route handlers.
// Illustrative domain model; not an Apple/Google SDK type.
type Feature = "workout_tracking" | "training_plans" | "analytics" | "coaching";
type Plan = "free" | "premium" | "pro";

const FEATURES: Record<Plan, ReadonlySet<Feature>> = {
  free: new Set(["workout_tracking"]),
  premium: new Set(["workout_tracking", "training_plans", "analytics"]),
  pro: new Set(["workout_tracking", "training_plans", "analytics", "coaching"]),
};

function includesFeature(plan: Plan, feature: Feature): boolean {
  return FEATURES[plan].has(feature);
}
A successful verification can create a grant with a source, start, and end. A paid term need not end when auto-renewal is canceled; an actual refund or revocation can change access sooner. Grace and billing-hold behaviors depend on store configuration and verified status.
{
  "userId": "usr_123",
  "plan": "premium",
  "features": ["workout_tracking", "training_plans", "analytics"],
  "verifiedAt": "2026-10-27T09:00:00Z",
  "accessUntil": "2026-11-27T09:00:00Z"
}
This response is your application's JSON, not a store response. It is a snapshot: the server must still enforce authorization and account for later revocations.
3. System architecture
```mermaid
flowchart LR
    I[iOS StoreKit] --> B[Subscription API]
    A[Android Play Billing] --> B
    B --> V[Store-specific verifier]
    V <--> S[(Apple / Google APIs)]
    V --> D[(PostgreSQL)]
    D --> E[Entitlement evaluator]
    E --> R[Protected fitness APIs]
    S --> N[Verified notification ingress]
    N --> Q[Durable queue]
    Q --> V
    T[Scheduled reconciliation] --> V
```
Purchase path: client initiates a platform purchase → obtains its legitimate transaction reference → authenticated backend receives it → platform verifier confirms app/product/status → database upsert binds purchase to the correct user → entitlements are recomputed → app fetches updated access.

Lifecycle path: store event → verify notification → durably enqueue → query current store state → update under transaction/lock → recompute grants. Notifications are signals to reconcile, not final entitlement decisions.
Suggested API contracts
