Authorization: Bearer <USER_SESSION_TOKEN>
Content-Type: application/json

{"platform":"google_play","purchaseReference":"<PURCHASE_TOKEN>"}
GET /v1/me/entitlements
Authorization: Bearer <USER_SESSION_TOKEN>
Identifiers and URLs above are application-defined endpoints. Never submit an API secret from the app.
4. Apple versus Google Play
Concern	Apple	Google Play
Client	StoreKit	Play Billing Library
Server verification	App Store Server API, signed transaction/renewal data	Google Play Developer API; purchases.subscriptionsv2.get
Typical purchase reference	Transaction or original transaction identifier	Purchase token
Subscription events	App Store Server Notifications V2 (signed JWS)	RTDN via Google Cloud Pub/Sub
Recovery	StoreKit transaction history/current entitlements and server APIs	Play Billing purchase query plus backend token verification
Important obligation	Verify signed data and correct subscription group/status	Acknowledge eligible purchases within Google's required window


Apple's Get All Subscription Statuses returns statuses for auto-renewable subscriptions; verify its signed transaction and renewal information. The API accepts a transaction identifier associated with the app. Apple signed V2 notifications use JWS. Do not merely Base64-decode a JWS and call it verified.

Google's subscriptionsv2.get retrieves current state for a purchase token. RTDN does not replace this lookup. Correctly acknowledge qualifying purchases, including relevant purchases originating outside the app; do not acknowledge pending transactions as completed.

Official references: StoreKit · App Store Server API · Apple subscription status endpoint · Play Billing integration · Google subscription lifecycle. Consult the current documentation when implementing versions, event types, and deadlines.
5. Purchase validation API
Use a platform adapter so application policy is independent of payment-provider response formats.
// Illustrative TypeScript interfaces and service logic.
type Platform = "apple" | "google_play";
type VerifiedPurchase = {
  platform: Platform;
  stableLineageKey: string;   // Derived with platform-specific rules
  transactionKey: string;
  appId: string;
  productId: string;
  storeState: string;
  paidThrough: Date | null;
  revoked: boolean;
  permitsAccess: boolean;     // Computed from VERIFIED platform data
};

interface StoreVerifier {
  verify(reference: string): Promise<VerifiedPurchase>;
}

interface SubscriptionRepository {
  // Implement with a real DB transaction and unique ownership constraint.
  applyVerifiedPurchase(userId: string, purchase: VerifiedPurchase): Promise<void>;
  getEntitlements(userId: string): Promise<unknown>;
}

async function verifyPurchase(
  authenticatedUserId: string,
  platform: Platform,
  reference: string,
  adapters: Record<Platform, StoreVerifier>,
  repo: SubscriptionRepository
) {
  if (!reference || reference.length > 8192) throw new Error("Invalid reference");
  const result = await adapters[platform].verify(reference);
  if (result.platform !== platform || result.appId !== EXPECTED_APP_IDS[platform]) {
    throw new Error("Application mismatch");
  }
  if (!APPROVED_PRODUCTS.has(result.productId)) throw new Error("Unknown product");
  // Reject if stableLineageKey is already bound to a different account.
  // Recompute or revoke grants according to verified status, not a client claim.
  await repo.applyVerifiedPurchase(authenticatedUserId, result);
  return repo.getEntitlements(authenticatedUserId);
}
EXPECTED_APP_IDS and APPROVED_PRODUCTS are server-configured allowlists. The repository must reject attempts to claim another user's purchase. For Google subscription replacements, preserve linked token history as appropriate; for Apple, account for subscription lineage and groups. Do not assume an original transaction ID and a Google purchase token mean the same thing.
Minimal route shape (Express)
// Illustrative only: authenticateUser and all dependencies are app-owned.
app.post("/v1/subscriptions/verify", authenticateUser, async (req, res) => {
  const { platform, purchaseReference } = req.body;
  if (platform !== "apple" && platform !== "google_play") {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "unsupported_platform" });
  }
  try {
    const entitlements = await verifyPurchase(
      req.user.id, platform, purchaseReference, storeAdapters, subscriptionRepo
    );
    return res.json(entitlements);
  } catch (err) {
    // Map account conflicts, invalid purchases and temporary store outages separately.
    return res.status(503).json({ error: "verification_unavailable_or_failed" });
  }
});
In production, validate JSON with a schema, limit body size, rate-limit the endpoint, classify errors accurately (400/403/409/503), and never log raw tokens.
6. Notification ingestion and reconciliation
Apple notifications must have their JWS signatures verified using the correct trust chain, app/environment checks, and current official validation procedures. Google Pub/Sub push requests must be authenticated according to the configured delivery mechanism; decode the Pub/Sub envelope, then query Google Play for authoritative subscription state. These are different verification mechanisms.
```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Store as Apple / Google
    participant Ingress as Notification endpoint
    participant Queue as Durable inbox / queue
    participant Worker as Reconciliation worker
    participant API as Platform API
    participant DB as DB + entitlements
    Store->>Ingress: Signed notification / authenticated Pub/Sub push
    Ingress->>Ingress: Verify origin, payload and context
    Ingress->>Queue: Insert unique event + enqueue atomically
    Ingress-->>Store: Acknowledge successful durable receipt
    Queue->>Worker: Deliver event (possibly repeated)
    Worker->>API: Fetch latest verified state
    API-->>Worker: Current subscription status
    Worker->>DB: Transactional upsert + recompute access
    Worker-->>Queue: Mark processed
```
// Illustrative; verification functions MUST be supplied by tested platform adapters.
async function ingestNotification(platform: Platform, request: unknown) {
  const verified = await notificationAdapters[platform].verifyIncoming(request);
  // Use real, validated platform event identity; don't hash arbitrary untrusted text.
  await inbox.insertOnceAndEnqueue({
    platform,
    eventKey: verified.eventKey,
    reference: verified.purchaseReference
  });
  return { accepted: true }; // Return correct HTTP acknowledgement for that transport.
}

async function processNotification(job: { platform: Platform; reference: string }) {
  const current = await storeAdapters[job.platform].verify(job.reference);
  await subscriptionRepo.applyStoreReconciliation(current);
  // Worker ACK only after committed update; retry transient failures with backoff.
}
Rules: (1) uniqueness on (platform, event_key); (2) durably persist before acknowledging; (3) retry temporary store API errors; (4) use a dead-letter queue and alerting; (5) serialize/constrain updates to the same subscription lineage; (6) reconcile periodically even when notifications appear healthy. Event order is not a reliable substitute for fetching current state.

For notification details: Apple V2 notification receipt and Google subscriptions / RTDN.
7. Database schema
Illustrative PostgreSQL schema; production needs migrations, environment partitioning, soft-delete/retention policies, additional indexes, historical lineage, and an outbox/inbox transaction strategy.
CREATE TABLE app_users (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY
);

CREATE TABLE store_products (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  platform TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (platform IN ('apple','google_play')),
  store_product_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  internal_plan TEXT NOT NULL,
  UNIQUE (platform, store_product_id)
);

CREATE TABLE subscriptions (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES app_users(id),
  platform TEXT NOT NULL,
  lineage_key TEXT NOT NULL,
  product_id UUID REFERENCES store_products(id),
  store_state TEXT NOT NULL,
  access_until TIMESTAMPTZ,
  last_verified_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  revoked BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE,
  UNIQUE (platform, lineage_key)
);

CREATE TABLE store_transactions (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  subscription_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES subscriptions(id),
  platform_transaction_key TEXT NOT NULL,
  occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
  UNIQUE (subscription_id, platform_transaction_key)
);

CREATE TABLE entitlement_grants (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  user_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES app_users(id),
  feature_code TEXT NOT NULL,
  source_subscription_id UUID REFERENCES subscriptions(id),
  effective_from TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  effective_until TIMESTAMPTZ
);

CREATE TABLE notification_inbox (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  platform TEXT NOT NULL,
  event_key TEXT NOT NULL,
  status TEXT NOT NULL,
  received_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  UNIQUE (platform, event_key)
);

CREATE TABLE reconciliation_audit (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  subscription_id UUID REFERENCES subscriptions(id),
  result TEXT NOT NULL,
  reconciled_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
Enforce one purchase lineage → one application owner. Reassignment requires an explicit, audited recovery workflow. Store platform environment (sandbox versus production) in a production design to avoid identifier collisions and mixing test data.
8. Entitlement authorization
Do not assume that returning premium: true in the mobile app protects premium coaching APIs.
// Illustrative middleware: store policy and verification freshness are app-specific.
function requireFeature(feature: Feature) {
  return async (req: any, res: any, next: any) => {
    const grants = await entitlementService.forUser(req.user.id);
    if (!grants.activeFeatures.includes(feature)) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: "subscription_required", feature });
    }
    next();
  };
}

app.get("/v1/coaching/program", authenticateUser, requireFeature("coaching"), handler);
Protect exported workout plans, AI-generated premium reports, and trainer workflows on the server. Cache verified entitlements with a clearly bounded lifetime and a strategy to invalidate them on revocation. Server-side authorization remains required even if the user interface is cached for offline display.
9. Difficult lifecycle cases
Situation	Correct approach
User cancels auto-renewal	Keep access through the verified paid-through date unless otherwise revoked
Renewal payment fails	Follow confirmed store status; grace and account hold differ by platform/configuration
Refund or revocation	Reverify and revoke affected grants promptly
Duplicate or out-of-order event	Deduplicate event, then reconcile latest current state
App reinstall	Recover eligible store purchases and query user-linked entitlements
Different app account claims purchase	Deny silent takeover; use audited support/recovery flow
Google token changes on plan replacement	Preserve and resolve linked token / purchase lineage
Store API unavailable	Retry verification and mark state stale; do not invent a new paid grant
Client offline during purchase	Retry backend verification when connectivity returns
Upgrade or downgrade	Apply verified store-specific effective timing; don't assume immediate tier switch


For short-lived verifier outages, decide explicitly whether to continue existing, recently verified entitlements for a narrow tolerance interval. Record the security and revenue trade-off; never authorize a new paid subscription from an unverified client assertion.
10. Security and privacy
- Secrets: keep Apple API signing credentials, Google service credentials, and Pub/Sub configuration server-side; rotate and scope access.
- Webhooks: verify Apple JWS and appropriate Google Pub/Sub transport identity before processing; defend against replay.
- API security: authenticate clients, enforce ownership, rate-limit verification/restore operations, and validate payloads.
- Logging: log correlation IDs, normalized states, retry counts, and errors—not raw purchase tokens, identity credentials, or fitness/health histories.
- Queue reliability: use retries, dead-letter queues, processing metrics, and reconciliation jobs.
- Data separation: store minimal subscription metadata away from workout, injury, nutrition, and health profiles wherever feasible.
- Privacy: HIPAA and GDPR applicability depends on actual business relationships, territory, processing purposes, and legal roles; billing code alone establishes no legal compliance.
